course: dictionary
lesson: 13146
title: Fore and paw: a word in parts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A forepaw is the front foot of an animal that has paws, such as a dog, cat, bear or squirrel. You can work out much of the meaning from its two parts: fore means front, as in forehead, and paw means foot.
explain: «The squirrel held a nut in its forepaws and nibbled it.» The squirrel holds the nut up in front, using its front feet like hands. Ask: what word would fit here? Try foot in the sentence instead: "held a nut in its front feet" still makes sense, so forepaw means front foot.
explain: Writers choose forepaw to show exactly which foot they mean. «The badger used its strong forepaws to dig a deep hole.» Digging tells you the forepaws are doing the hard work.
fact: Forepaw is a noun. One is a forepaw, and two are forepaws.
fact: Fore means front, and paw means foot, so a forepaw is a front foot.
fact: Animals use their forepaws for holding food, digging, grooming and catching.
fact: «The squirrel held a nut in its forepaws.» Squirrels hold food with their forepaws.
fact: «The badger used its forepaws to dig.» Badgers dig with their strong forepaws.
exercise: «The squirrel held a nut in its forepaws.» What is a forepaw? | A nut | *A front foot of an animal that has paws | A tail | A kind of tree
exercise: «The badger used its forepaws to dig a deep hole.» What were the forepaws used for? | Singing | Swimming across a river | Reading a map | *Digging
exercise: «The dog lifted one forepaw and looked up at me.» Find and copy the word that means a front foot. | looked | *forepaw | me | dog
test: «The cat held up her hurt forepaw.» Why might the cat hold up a forepaw? | She is fast asleep | She is going to sing a song | Cats have no feet at all | *Her front foot may be hurt
test: Which word is a noun for a front foot of an animal? | foreground | *forepaw | forehead | forest
exam: «The dog lifted one forepaw and looked up, as if asking for a treat.» What is the dog most likely asking for? | A long walk in the rain | A new collar | A bath | *A treat
